n status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.thm-largest-skip-late"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.thm-largest-skip-late"],"id":"long.thm-largest-skip-late","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.6 (Keeping the largest skipped rank beyond two thirds)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.6, p. 46, label thm:largest-skip-late","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Let $D_s\\subseteq\\{2,\\ldots,s-1\\}$ be the support of the integer-greedy row defined in Section 5.4. Assume the following implication for every $s\\ge14$ and every largest omitted rank $d=\\max(\\{2,\\ldots,s-1\\}\\smallsetminus D_s)$:\n$$\n2s<3d\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n 2(s+1)<3d\\quad\\text{or}\\quad s\\notin D_{s+1}.\n$$\nThen $1/2\\in\\mathcal A$. The hypothesis says that either the same omitted rank remains beyond two thirds of the next row, or the next row omits its terminal rank. The latter alternative is exactly an upper or middle transition in the preceding classification.*\n\n*The cited finite calculation establishes the initial inequality at row $14$. The displayed implication then preserves the inequality by induction and supplies omitted ranks tending to infinity. It is an unproved condition on every later row; the verified base case alone does not establish it.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-largest-skip-late","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-largest-skip-late","id":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-largest-skip-late%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.LargestSkipLateAt%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-largest-skip-late%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.LargestSkipLateAt%22%2C%22f